course: dictionary
lesson: 10161
title: Thunderstorms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A thunderstorm is a storm with thunder and lightning, usually with heavy rain and strong winds. The sentences around the word give clues: «Thunderstorm clouds reach so high that their tops are far below freezing, even in summer.» So the clouds are very tall.
explain: You see lightning before you hear thunder. «In a thunderstorm the light of the lightning reaches you almost at once, but the sound of thunder travels about one kilometre in three seconds.» Light is much faster than sound, so the flash comes first and the bang follows later.
explain: The Queen was «frowning like a thunderstorm». A frown is an angry face, and a thunderstorm is dark and stormy, so the simile tells you she looked very cross.
explain: Staying safe matters most. When thunder roars, go indoors. The safest place is inside a building or a car with a metal roof, not under a tree or in an open field.
fact: A thunderstorm is a storm with thunder and lightning, usually with heavy rain and strong winds.
fact: Lightning is a giant electric spark, and thunder is the sound of air heated by it.
fact: You can estimate how far away a storm is by counting the seconds between the flash and the bang, then dividing by 3 to get kilometres.
fact: «Thunderstorm» is a noun, and you can also say «thunderstorms» for more than one.
fact: The safest place in a thunderstorm is inside a building or a car with a metal roof.
exercise: «In a thunderstorm the light of the lightning reaches you almost at once.» Which reaches you first? | *Lightning | Wind | Thunder | Rain
exercise: Counting the seconds between a flash and a bang tells you what? | The temperature of the rain | The name of the storm | *How far away the storm is | How tall the clouds are
exercise: When thunder roars, where should you go? | *Indoors | Under a tall tree | Next to a metal fence | Across an open field
test: Sound from a storm travels about one kilometre in three seconds. If you count 9 seconds, about how far away is the storm? | *3 kilometres | 1 kilometre | 27 kilometres | 9 kilometres
test: Which is the safest place in a thunderstorm? | In an open field | Beside a metal fence | *Inside a building | Under a tree
exam: «Suddenly the Queen stands in front of them, frowning like a thunderstorm.» What does this suggest about the Queen? | Sleepy and bored | Happy and calm | Lost and tired | *Angry and cross
